# PythOS boot configuration template (Limine v9, `limine` protocol).
#
# The build generates one concrete limine.conf per ISO by substituting
# @DEFAULT_ENTRY@ with the index of the entry that should boot:
#
#   1 -> normal boot        build/pythos.iso            exit 33
#   2 -> panic test         build/pythos-panic.iso      exit 35
#   3 -> breakpoint (#BP)   build/pythos-int3.iso       exit 33 (handler returns)
#   4 -> page fault (#PF)   build/pythos-pf.iso         exit 35
#   5 -> double fault (#DF) build/pythos-df.iso         exit 35 (handled on IST1)
#   6 -> frame allocator    build/pythos-memtest.iso    exit 33
#   7 -> double free        build/pythos-doublefree.iso exit 35
#   8 -> virtual memory     build/pythos-vmtest.iso     exit 33
#   9 -> stale TLB          build/pythos-staletlb.iso   exit 33 (advisory)
#  10 -> guard page         build/pythos-guardpage.iso  exit 35
#  11 -> NX enforcement     build/pythos-nx.iso         exit 35
#  12 -> stack overflow     build/pythos-stackovf.iso   exit 35
#  13 -> kernel heap        build/pythos-heaptest.iso   exit 33
#  14 -> heap exhaustion    build/pythos-heapoom.iso    exit 35
#  15 -> heap guard page    build/pythos-heapguard.iso  exit 35
#  16..19 -> M5 capability scenarios
#  20..23 -> M6 thread scenarios
#  24 -> address spaces     build/pythos-vspacetest.iso exit 33
#  25 -> kernel-half map    build/pythos-vskernel.iso   exit 33 (refused, not fatal)
#  26 -> isolation          build/pythos-vsisolation.iso exit 35
#  27 -> revoked vspace     build/pythos-vsstale.iso    exit 35
#  28 -> ring 3             build/pythos-usertest.iso   exit 33
#  29 -> privileged insn    build/pythos-userpriv.iso   exit 35
#  30 -> kernel read        build/pythos-userkread.iso  exit 35
#  31 -> SMEP               build/pythos-usersmep.iso   exit 35
#  32 -> SMAP               build/pythos-usersmap.iso   exit 35
#  33 -> forged capability  build/pythos-userforge.iso  exit 33 (refused, not fatal)
#  34 -> IPC rendezvous     build/pythos-ipctest.iso    exit 33
#  35 -> cancellation cost  build/pythos-ipccancel.iso  exit 33 (measurement)
#  36 -> revoked endpoint   build/pythos-ipcrevoked.iso exit 33 (woken, not fatal)
#
# Every ISO embeds the exact same kernel.elf. The fault paths are therefore
# exercised on the shipping binary rather than on a special build, which is
# the only way the tests say anything about what we actually ship.
#
# timeout: 0 boots the default entry instantly and keeps menu chatter out of
# the serial log, which the test runner parses.

timeout: 0
default_entry: @DEFAULT_ENTRY@
verbose: no

/PythOS (normal boot)
    comment: Prints boot info, then exits QEMU with host status 33.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf

/PythOS (panic test)
    comment: Same kernel; cmdline triggers a deliberate panic (host status 35).
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: panic-test

/PythOS (breakpoint exception)
    comment: int3 -> #BP. A trap, so the handler returns and we exit 33.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: fault-int3

/PythOS (page fault)
    comment: Write to an unmapped canonical address -> #PF, CR2 reported, exit 35.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: fault-pf

/PythOS (double fault)
    comment: Corrupt RSP then fault -> #DF handled on IST1, exit 35.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: fault-df

/PythOS (frame allocator tests)
    comment: Bulk alloc/free, leak check and exhaustion/recovery. Exit 33.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: mem-test

/PythOS (double free)
    comment: Free the same frame twice; the allocator must panic. Exit 35.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: mem-double-free

/PythOS (virtual memory tests)
    comment: map/unmap/translate, error cases, reclaim survived. Exit 33.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: vm-test

/PythOS (stale TLB check)
    comment: Unmap without invalidating. Advisory -- QEMU's TLB is not real
    comment: hardware, so a missing fault is reported, not failed. Exit 33.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: vm-stale-tlb

/PythOS (guard page)
    comment: Write below the IST stack; the guard page must fault. Exit 35.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: vm-guard-page

/PythOS (NX enforcement)
    comment: Jump to a data page in PMAP; NX must fault. Exit 35.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: vm-nx

/PythOS (main stack overflow)
    comment: Recurse off the bottom of the kernel's own stack. Unlike the
    comment: guard page test, which writes at the guard directly, this
    comment: arrives the way a real overflow would -- and escalates to #DF,
    comment: because delivering the #PF would need the stack that just ran
    comment: out. Handled on IST1. Exit 35.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: vm-stack-overflow

/PythOS (kernel heap tests)
    comment: Box/Vec/BTreeMap/String, 10000 sized allocations with canaries,
    comment: growth, and a return to the baseline byte count. Exit 33.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: heap-test

/PythOS (heap exhaustion)
    comment: Allocate 1 MiB at a time and leak it until the heap hits its
    comment: limit or physical memory runs out. Either way: a panic naming
    comment: the size and alignment, never a silent null. Exit 35.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: heap-oom

/PythOS (heap guard page)
    comment: Write to the unmapped page above the heap's maximum extent.
    comment: #PF with CR2 in the guard. Exit 35.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: heap-guard

/PythOS (capability system tests)
    comment: Object sizes against ADR 017's ceilings, the rights lattice
    comment: exhaustively, retype and its atomicity, copy and the refusal
    comment: to widen rights, every CPtr failure mode, the CPtr invariant
    comment: the first draft of ADR 018 broke, the depth cap, device
    comment: Untyped, and delete against revoke. Exit 33.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: cap-test

/PythOS (revoke cost measurement)
    comment: Build the widest, deepest derivation tree the init CSpace can
    comment: hold and time a revoke over it. Not pass/fail: ADR 019 put the
    comment: worst case at ~56 ms and never checked it, so this reports a
    comment: per-capability cost to write back into the ADR. Exit 33.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: cap-revoke-depth

/PythOS (allocation from an interrupt)
    comment: Allocate a frame from interrupt context. ADR 022 made "handlers
    comment: do not allocate" an enforced rule rather than a lock that would
    comment: protect nothing on one CPU; this is the tripwire firing. M6
    comment: enables interrupts and will need it. Exit 35.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: cap-interrupt-alloc

/PythOS (allocation after retirement)
    comment: Call the frame allocator after boot retired it. From the end of
    comment: boot memory comes only from retyping an Untyped, so a direct
    comment: allocation is a path that was meant to be capability-mediated
    comment: and is not. Exit 35.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: cap-retired-allocator

/PythOS (thread and scheduler tests)
    comment: Two kernel threads on their own stacks hand off 100 times;
    comment: the LAPIC timer fires and preempts a thread that never
    comment: yields; suspend, resume and ReadRegisters work; and the
    comment: interrupt flag is where ADR 025 says it is in each of the
    comment: three kinds of protected region. Exit 33.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: thread-test

/PythOS (thread stack overflow)
    comment: A thread recurses off the bottom of its own stack -- one
    comment: retyped from an Untyped and mapped for its TCB, not the
    comment: main stack M3 tested. The report names the thread. Exit 35.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: thread-stack-overflow

/PythOS (allocation inside a real interrupt)
    comment: M5 tested ADR 022's tripwire by driving the depth counter
    comment: by hand, because there were no interrupts. This calls
    comment: allocate() from inside the timer handler the LAPIC
    comment: dispatched to. Exit 35.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: thread-interrupt-alloc

/PythOS (operate on a running thread)
    comment: ADR 026: a thread the scheduler is holding cannot have its
    comment: TCB rewritten -- ThreadBusy, not undefined behaviour. Then
    comment: Suspend, and the same call succeeds: the refusal is a
    comment: sequencing requirement, not a dead end. Exit 35.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: thread-kill-running

/PythOS (address space tests)
    comment: A user VSpace built entirely out of capabilities: a root
    comment: PageTable, three intermediate tables the caller supplies,
    comment: and a Frame. Then the check that cannot be faked -- two
    comment: threads reading the same virtual address and seeing
    comment: different bytes. Plus rights narrowing into permissions,
    comment: unmap returning both the Frame and the table to their
    comment: holders, and the cost of a CR3 reload measured against a
    comment: switch that skips one. Exit 33.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: vspace-test

/PythOS (mapping into the kernel half)
    comment: Four attempts to map a Frame at a kernel address through a
    comment: user VSpace. Every one must be refused with NotUserAddress:
    comment: the kernel's top-level entries are shared by every address
    comment: space, so such a mapping would edit tables that every other
    comment: VSpace walks (ADR 027, K1). A caller error, not a kernel
    comment: fault -- so this one exits 33, not 35.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: vspace-kernel-half

/PythOS (address space isolation)
    comment: Isolation from the failing side. An address is mapped in
    comment: one VSpace and not the other; a thread in the second one
    comment: reads it and must take a #PF with CR2 at that address.
    comment: vspace-test shows two threads seeing different values;
    comment: this shows that reaching across is not merely discouraged.
    comment: Exit 35.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: vspace-isolation

/PythOS (revoked address space)
    comment: ADR 028's two cases. Revoking the VSpace in CR3 right now is
    comment: refused -- the CPU would walk page tables handed back to
    comment: their Untyped. Revoking an idle one succeeds, and the thread
    comment: bound to it is suspended when something next tries to switch
    comment: into it, rather than silently dropped into the kernel's
    comment: address space. Exit 35.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: vspace-stale-cr3

/PythOS (ring 3 tests)
    comment: A thread running at CPL 3, in its own address space, with
    comment: code in a page that is executable and not writable. It reads
    comment: CS and reports the privilege level through the one syscall;
    comment: two such threads read the same virtual address and see
    comment: different bytes, enter the kernel on different stacks, and
    comment: keep their own XMM registers across preemption. The fxsave
    comment: count equals the switch count, which is what shows nothing
    comment: is lazy. Exit 33.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: user-test

/PythOS (privileged instruction from ring 3)
    comment: User code executes cli, then hlt, rdmsr and lgdt. Each is
    comment: privileged, so the first one must raise #GP -- and cli is
    comment: first deliberately, because it is the one that looks
    comment: harmless. Exit 35.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: user-privileged

/PythOS (user reads the kernel half)
    comment: Ring 3 reads an address in the kernel heap. #PF with the
    comment: USER bit set in the error code -- that bit is what tells a
    comment: user overreach from a kernel pointer bug, and the two mean
    comment: entirely different things. Exit 35.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: user-kernel-read

/PythOS (SMEP)
    comment: The kernel jumps into a user code page -- the *same* page
    comment: ring 3 has just executed successfully, so the fault cannot
    comment: be explained by a mapping difference. #PF with the
    comment: instruction-fetch bit set. SMEP has been in CR4 since M3
    comment: with nothing to protect; this is its first real test.
    comment: Exit 35.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: user-smep

/PythOS (SMAP)
    comment: The kernel reads a user page twice: once holding a
    comment: UserAccess guard, which must succeed, and once without,
    comment: which must fault. Both halves in one scenario, because
    comment: either alone proves half the claim. The guarded read runs
    comment: first -- the unguarded one ends the VM. Exit 35.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: user-smap

/PythOS (forged capability pointers)
    comment: The first genuinely hostile input this kernel has taken.
    comment: Ring 3 invokes with reserved bits set, an out-of-range
    comment: index, a stale generation, an empty slot, a real object of
    comment: the wrong type, and an unknown method. Each must come back
    comment: with its *own* error -- six InvalidArguments would satisfy
    comment: a count and prove nothing -- and the kernel must still be
    comment: running afterwards. Exit 33.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: user-cap-forge

/PythOS (IPC rendezvous)
    comment: M9a. Two threads meet on an endpoint, in both orders: a
    comment: sender that arrives first blocks until somebody receives,
    comment: and a receiver that arrives first blocks until somebody
    comment: sends. The message survives the sender being descheduled,
    comment: which is what ADR 033 means by "the message lives in the
    comment: sender's TCB" -- an endpoint holds no storage at all.
    comment: Also: 0, 3 and 128-word messages, every tag field the
    comment: sender controls refused by its own check, and three
    comment: queued senders delivered in the order they arrived.
    comment: Exit 33.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: ipc-test

/PythOS (cancellation cost)
    comment: M9a's real result. Removing one waiter from a queue of 50
    comment: and then from a queue of 500, timed both times. One figure
    comment: would say nothing; two figures ten times apart, agreeing,
    comment: are what shows the cost does not depend on the length.
    comment: ADR 033 originally specified a singly linked queue -- as
    comment: M6's ready queue is -- and was corrected before any code
    comment: was written, because an endpoint's queue length is an
    comment: *adversary's* thread count and the walk happens with
    comment: interrupts disabled. This is that correction, measured.
    comment: Exit 33.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: ipc-cancel-cost

/PythOS (revoked endpoint)
    comment: Three threads wait on an endpoint; its capability is
    comment: deleted. All three must wake with EndpointDead, and the
    comment: kernel must still be running -- so this exits 33, not 35.
    comment: Woken rather than the delete being refused, and the
    comment: reasoning is ADR 039's rather than ADR 026's: there,
    comment: refusing was safe because the thread's own owner could
    comment: suspend it first. Here the waiters may belong to other
    comment: components, so refusing would let any of them make an
    comment: endpoint un-revokable.
    comment: This is also what proves cancel_ipc is wired into
    comment: cnode::delete at all -- without the call, the scenario
    comment: hangs. Exit 33.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: ipc-endpoint-revoked

/PythOS (call and reply)
    comment: M9b. Four scenarios: a Call/Reply round trip, ReplyRecv
    comment: doing two operations in one kernel entry, two badged
    comment: clients the server tells apart, and the reply-stealing
    comment: case.
    comment: The fourth is ADR 036's finest decision: reply_to is
    comment: written at the rendezvous rather than when Recv returns,
    comment: because otherwise a second server arriving between a
    comment: first server's Recv and its Reply would take the first
    comment: server's caller. The window is opened by the test rather
    comment: than by a timer, so the scenario is deterministic --
    comment: ADR 023's K8, learned in M9a. Exit 33.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: ipc-call-test

/PythOS (server dies owing a reply)
    comment: A server is revoked while holding a caller. The client
    comment: must wake with ReplyTargetGone rather than waiting for
    comment: an answer that can never come.
    comment: ADR 036 separates two cases and treats them differently:
    comment: a server that *declines* to answer leaves its client
    comment: waiting forever, which is accepted because a timeout
    comment: needs a scheduling budget this project does not have. A
    comment: server that *dies* is a case the kernel can see, so
    comment: leaving the client blocked would be a defect rather than
    comment: a trade-off. Exit 33.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: ipc-server-dies

/PythOS (stale reply target)
    comment: The caller's TCB is revoked and a new TCB is retyped into
    comment: the same memory -- which ADR 020's watermark reset makes
    comment: likely rather than contrived. The server's Reply must be
    comment: refused.
    comment: This is the only scenario in which the generation check
    comment: can be seen to work: the address matches, so an
    comment: address-only check would deliver the reply to a stranger.
    comment: The test verifies the new TCB really did land at the old
    comment: address BEFORE asserting anything, because if it did not
    comment: the scenario proves nothing and must say so. Exit 33.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: ipc-stale-reply

/PythOS (self-call deadlock)
    comment: A thread Calls its own endpoint with nobody receiving. It
    comment: blocks forever, and that is deliberately not prevented: a
    comment: check for "am I the receiver" would catch this and miss
    comment: A->B->A, which is partial protection at full cost.
    comment: What the design does promise is that the deadlock is
    comment: escapable, and this tests that promise -- Suspend takes
    comment: the thread off the queue and the Call returns an error.
    comment: The escape needs a TCB capability, so rescue is itself an
    comment: authority. Exit 33.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: ipc-self-call

/PythOS (IPC cost)
    comment: ADR 040 refused a fastpath and promised to measure the
    comment: slow one instead. Three figures: a bare Send+Recv
    comment: rendezvous, a Call+Reply round, and a server loop built
    comment: on ReplyRecv.
    comment: The differences matter more than any single number -- a
    comment: Call is two rendezvous where a Send is one, and the test
    comment: asserts that ordering rather than a target, because equal
    comment: figures would mean the measurement is reporting
    comment: something other than the IPC path.
    comment: Minimum of 32 samples after a warm-up, which is M9a's
    comment: lesson: the first touch of freshly-zeroed memory swamped
    comment: that measurement by two orders of magnitude. Exit 33.
    protocol: limine
    path: boot():/boot/kernel.elf
    cmdline: ipc-cost